Yes. Beverage Goblin has a strong product shape because the Spotify analogy gives you the interaction model almost immediately, while beverages give you several things music doesn’t have: taste, ritual, location, inventory, price, seasonality, and physical purchase.

The simplest product thesis is:

Spotify organizes what you listen to. Beverage Goblin organizes what you drink.

But I’d make one important distinction: don’t build it as a beverage database with social features bolted on. Build it around the personal beverage identity of the user. Your Goblin gradually learns your palate, routines, favorites, curiosities, price sensitivity, and what you drink in different situations.

